                <h2>Introduction: The True Cost of an Interruption</h2>
<p>A utility app team was pushing hard during a critical growth phase when the finance team went offline for the evening. Unable to recharge the account in time, their primary campaign paused for 3 hours. The damage extended beyond lost impressions—Facebook's algorithm spent that time relearning the audience model, and the next day's CPA jumped 22%.</p>
<p>This isn't unique. During active scaling phases, account interruptions cost more than just ad exposure; they destabilize algorithm performance. This SOP establishes an automated balance alert system that shifts your team from "money waits for people" to "money waits for campaigns."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Build a Multi-Level Alert System</h2>
<h3>3.1 Define Alert Thresholds</h3>
<p>Different account sizes and spend velocities require different thresholds. Core principle: ensure recharge arrival time exceeds the time from alert trigger to balance depletion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Threshold Formula:</strong> Alert Line = Daily Spend × Recharge Arrival Days + Safety Buffer (0.5-1 day recommended)</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Daily Spend</th>
<th>Level 1 Alert</th>
<th>Level 2 Alert</th>
<th>Urgent Alert</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Under $500</td>
<td>$1500</td>
<td>$800</td>
<td>$300</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>$500-$2000</td>
<td>$5000</td>
<td>$2500</td>
<td>$1000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Above $2000</td>
<td>2-day spend</td>
<td>1-day spend</td>
<td>6-hour spend</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>3.2 Configure Alert Notification Channels</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Level 1</strong>: Email → Finance Lead + Operations Lead</li>
<li><strong>Level 2</strong>: Email + Slack/WorkWeChat Group → Full Delivery Team</li>
<li><strong>Urgent</strong>: Phone + SMS + Email → All Stakeholders</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Set Up Automated Monitoring</h2>
<h3>3.3 Connect to Balance Monitoring Tools</h3>
<p>Complete these settings in the Yiyu backend:</p>
<ol>
<li>Navigate to "Account Management" → "Balance Monitoring"</li>
<li>Add ad accounts to monitor (Facebook, Google, TikTok supported)</li>
<li>Set alert thresholds (refer to the table above, then adjust based on actual spend)</li>
<li>Configure notification channels and recipients</li>
<li>Enable automatic balance screenshots (9 AM and 3 PM daily)</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.4 Establish Recharge Response SLAs</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert Level</th>
<th>Response Time</th>
<th>Action Required</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Level 1</td>
<td>Within 4 hours</td>
<td>Ops initiates recharge, Finance approves</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Level 2</td>
<td>Within 2 hours</td>
<td>Finance prioritizes, Ops prepares alternatives</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Urgent</td>
<td>Within 30 minutes</td>
<td>Immediate recharge, pause lower-priority Campaigns</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Develop Contingency Plans</h2>
<h3>3.5 Campaign Priority Strategy for Emergencies</h3>
<p>When balance is depleting and recharge cannot arrive in time, pause campaigns in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Preserve First</strong>: Top 20% ROAS performers of the day</li>
<li><strong>Can Pause</strong>: New creative testing campaigns (high spend, unstable conversion)</li>
<li><strong>Last Resort</strong>: Brand campaigns (typically fixed budget, less affected by short-term fluctuations)</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Pre-mark Campaign priority levels in your ad platform backend for quick emergency actions.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>3.6 Pre-Charge Account Strategy</h3>
<p>For accounts with stable spend patterns, adopt a "pre-charge + reserve fund" approach:</p>
<ul>
<li>Maintain balance no lower than 3 days of spend</li>
<li>Keep $500-1000 in a reserve fund for emergencies</li>
<li>Finance checks all account balances every Wednesday and Friday</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Review and Optimize</h2>
<h3>3.7 Weekly Balance Management Review Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check number and frequency of triggered alerts this week</li>
<li>[ ] Analyze recharge arrival times—is there room for improvement?</li>
<li>[ ] Evaluate if current thresholds match actual spend</li>
<li>[ ] Review emergency interruption cases—update contingency plans</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: How do I set unified alerts for multiple accounts?</strong></p>
<p>A: Use Yiyu's multi-account balance monitoring feature to batch-set alert rules, with support for different thresholds per account group.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if recharge arrival times are unpredictable?</strong></p>
<p>A: Communicate with your account manager about opening a priority recharge channel, and factor in the longest possible arrival time when calculating safety buffers.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if sudden high spend causes alerts to fail?</strong></p>
<p>A: Beyond fixed thresholds, enable "spend velocity alerts"—notify immediately when hourly spend exceeds 2× the daily average.</p>
